  "historical_provenance_note": "script_sha256 / tool_sha256 is stamped once, at render time, into the record the script produced. It is NOT re-derived by any test, and superseded values are legitimately committed: as of this registry eight of these ten scripts have drifted from the digests their records carry (current_bytes_recorded: false); two are exceptions. fixtures/render_fixture.py was flipped to true by #126 when the SXT-028f fixture bundles were re-rendered under the pinned oracle, and stamped a second time by #136's SXT-028e-sse re-render. fixtures/render_slfo_fixture.py, added by SXT-041 (#75), reads true from the start -- it is not one of the #269 eight, and where fixtures/render_fixture.py regained resolvability by being re-rendered, this entry's never lapsed: this leaf's records were rendered from the file as committed, so its stamp currently resolves. A live-equality assertion on these would still be wrong -- nothing re-derives a script_sha256 stamp, and no entry here carries a sha256 field. Editing one does not invalidate a record; it does mean the record's provenance digest no longer resolves to any file in the tree, so re-rendering is the only way to restore a resolvable stamp. Disposition of the lint findings in these scripts, decided explicitly in #269 (not inherited from #254's source table, one row of which does not hold): they are ordinary code, so a dead-code finding in one is cleanable with no registry ceremony and no leaf re-run -- the per-entry lint_finding_disposition field records that decision for each, including the ninth entry added by SXT-041 and the tenth added by #447. For the eight entries that still read false the cost is only that an edit moves the file further from the digests its records carry, and that cost is already spent: those flags were false before #269's cleanup and were re-derived false after it, in the same change. fixtures/render_fixture.py and fixtures/render_slfo_fixture.py no longer qualify for that -- each has a currently resolvable stamp (#126/#136 re-renders for the former, the as-committed SXT-041 render for the latter), so an edit to either does spend something; their per-entry dispositions record that, and neither is a live pin. This says nothing about live_pins, where a finding stays permanently kept.",
